00:09:24.590 --> 00:09:26.480
It's someone going on and being like,

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:28.890
hey world, I've got a thing,
want to give me money for it?

00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:29.870
Do you trust me?

00:09:29.870 --> 00:09:31.380
Is this going to be an interaction?

00:09:31.380 --> 00:09:32.450
- You understand communication.

00:09:32.450 --> 00:09:35.150
- Yeah, so I realized that the reason...

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:37.460
From that first campaign
we had 188 backers

00:09:37.460 --> 00:09:39.331
and the second campaign we had over 3000,

00:09:39.331 --> 00:09:42.010
I think, if memory serves, and in between

00:09:42.010 --> 00:09:43.300
those two periods of time I realized

00:09:43.300 --> 00:09:46.060
that those first 188 people did not open

00:09:46.060 --> 00:09:47.610
their wallets to us because they...

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:48.813
Actually, wallets was
one of the rewards...

00:09:48.813 --> 00:09:51.220
Because they wanted a wallet from me.

00:09:51.220 --> 00:09:53.060
They opened their wallets to us because

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:54.767
they were like, "This
is such a great story,

00:09:54.767 --> 00:09:57.230
"this person in design
school with this idea."

00:09:57.230 --> 00:10:00.550
And so 18 months later I
was even better at that,

00:10:00.550 --> 00:10:03.527
told a really powerful story and then--

00:10:03.527 --> 00:10:05.950
- And that's the theme of
your business now, right?

00:10:05.950 --> 00:10:09.000
Like, even the meta of
this, when we were kind of

00:10:09.000 --> 00:10:10.520
doing our thing, you're like,
"I want to be on the show,"

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:11.353
and I'm like, you know what?

00:10:11.353 --> 00:10:13.430
Let's do it, mainly on the fact that you

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:15.350
were smart enough to
know the value of what

00:10:15.350 --> 00:10:17.288
you're doing right this minute in itself

00:10:17.288 --> 00:10:20.270
was something I wanted to reward because

00:10:20.270 --> 00:10:22.520
it's almost like you want
to give back to the game.

00:10:22.520 --> 00:10:25.344
Because I also understand
it, if that makes sense.

00:10:25.344 --> 00:10:27.420
- There's a meta to this for sure,

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:29.640
which is like, at each
juncture in my career

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:31.960
it's been like how do you learn a thing

00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:33.770
actually live the thing, build a tool

00:10:33.770 --> 00:10:35.120
for other people to do the thing.

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:37.730
So I launched a product in Kickstarter,

00:10:37.730 --> 00:10:39.800
took it to retail, sold
it all over the world,

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:42.840
and it was fucking painful, right?

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:45.700
It was hard, and rough, and
not good, in so many ways.

00:10:45.700 --> 00:10:46.820
We didn't make money with it,

00:10:46.820 --> 00:10:49.200
it was a success in the sense that

00:10:50.250 --> 00:10:51.840
we shut it down intentionally,

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:54.640
that's sometimes what a success is.

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:57.130
And so we did that, but
in the course of that

00:10:57.130 --> 00:10:59.940
we learned how to navigate the environment

00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:03.130
of custom manufactured
packaging, how hard it was

00:11:03.130 --> 00:11:04.960
to manage a supply chain for a business

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:06.700
with physical product, and we were like,

00:11:06.700 --> 00:11:08.410
this is something we can solve for.

00:11:08.410 --> 00:11:11.960
That was four years ago
now, and now we make

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:14.240
packaging for a lot of
the big e-com brands

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:15.720
that you know of, and the reason why

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:16.820
is not because--
- Like?

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:17.653
- Lumi's cool...

00:11:17.653 --> 00:11:21.823
Um, Casper, FabFitFun, Parachute Home,

00:11:23.099 --> 00:11:26.199
Allbirds, MeUndies, yeah,
companies like that.

00:11:26.199 --> 00:11:28.840
The reason why we work with them is not

00:11:28.840 --> 00:11:30.680
because our packaging's so cool,

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:33.520
actually it's really that supply chain

00:11:33.520 --> 00:11:35.913
gets really complicated, and packaging--

00:11:35.913 --> 00:11:38.020
- You're solving a problem.

00:11:38.020 --> 00:11:39.497
- Packaging is different...

00:11:39.497 --> 00:11:41.970
The plain cardboard box used to just be

00:11:41.970 --> 00:11:43.050
something that gets something from point A

00:11:43.050 --> 00:11:45.630
to point B, now it's going to doorsteps,

00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:47.770
millions of doorsteps every single day.

00:11:47.770 --> 00:11:50.260
That means the challenges
the boxes are under

00:11:50.260 --> 00:11:51.506
are different then ever before.

00:11:51.506 --> 00:11:53.860
The packaging world
and manufacturing world

00:11:53.860 --> 00:11:56.170
is really grappling with
what those changes mean

00:11:56.170 --> 00:11:58.020
and we're helping solve those problems,

00:11:58.020 --> 00:11:59.330
but it's kind of...

00:11:59.330 --> 00:12:00.950
I mean, I'm me, but it's kind of profound,

00:12:00.950 --> 00:12:04.150
we're in a profound moment of how...

00:12:04.150 --> 00:12:05.590
What packaging means to everyone,

00:12:05.590 --> 00:12:07.440
because you couldn't even order something

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:08.820
like this directly to your doorstep

00:12:08.820 --> 00:12:10.370
so relatively recently.

00:12:10.370 --> 00:12:11.203
